BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Senderos: Los Wappo en Wilikos
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261107T104500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261107T130000
UID:261026x7hlQpCIk0i57WFL8BPD6A
DESCRIPTION:\\[Translated to English below\\] Aprende acerca de las histor
 ias y tradiciones de la tribu Wappo\, una de las tribus originarias de est
 a área\, en esta caminata familiar de 2 millas. Nos reuniremos en el esta
 cionamiento del granero blanco (el White Barn) y caminaremos el sendero Me
 adow mientras escuchamos acerca de la historia cultural de los Wappo en un
 a de sus aldeas ancestral\, Wilikos\, hoy en día parte de Sugarloaf. \n\
 nEvento gratuito\; requiere registración. Hay transportación disponible 
 gratuita dentro de un radio de 20 millas al parque para familias que la ne
 cesiten. Para detalles y registración\, visita EVENT LINK\n\nAlternativam
 ente\, llama a Mario (707-387-4087)\, Alma (707-247-5767)\, entre martes y
  sábado) o escríbenos a senderos@sonomaecologycenter.org. ¿No puedes ac
 ompañarnos ese día? ¡Guarda la fecha el 5 de diciembre para más aventu
 ras!\n\nSenderos Naturales es parcialmente respaldado por una beca de Park
 s California.\n\nFotografía cortesía de Alma Shaw\n\n\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\
 \_\\_\\_\n\nLearn about the history and traditions of the Mishewal Wappo T
 ribe\, one of the original groups to live in this area. On this family hik
 e\, will walk approximately 2 miles along the Meadow trail while we listen
  to the cultural history of the Wappo peoples in one of their ancestral vi
 llages called Wilikios\, today found in Sugarloaf.\n\nThis event is planne
 d around a primarily Latino audience and will be conducted in Spanish. Fam
 ilies from underrepresented groups are welcome to join. Free transportatio
 n available within a 20 mile radius of the park for families in need. For 
 questions\, please email senderos@sonomaecologycenter.org.\n\nThis event i
 s partially supported by Route to Parks\, a project of Parks California.\n
 \nPhoto courtesy of Alma Shaw
LOCATION:Sugarloaf Ridge State Park
URL:https://sec.regfox.com/loswappo26
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:We Players: Mountain Goddess Ceremony at Western Hills Garden
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261107T150000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261107T180000
UID:261026SJiVW71lFk2adTISwQ_SVA
DESCRIPTION:Step into the Garden for an immersive ceremony of release\, re
 newal\, and connection to past\, present\, and future.\n\nConceived and di
 rected by Ava Roy and created and performed by Ling Ling Lee\, Mountain Go
 ddess invites participants into a sensory journey through Western Hills Ga
 rden. Guided by the Mountain Goddess and her spirited guardians\, Ox Head 
 and Horse Face\, guests will move through meditation\, exploration\, ritua
 l\, and play—drawing on ancestry\, memory\, the elements\, and the lands
 cape itself.\n\nThe experience culminates in a seasonal\, culturally relev
 ant meal prepared by acclaimed Sonoma County Chef Sean Quan\, featuring B
 eijing Zhajiangmian-style noodle bar with an array of different vegetables
 \, pickle\, and herb garnishes. Wine\, beer\, non-alcoholic drinks\, and c
 heerful company will also be provided.\n\nPresented by We Players in part
 nership with Western Hills Garden.\n\nConceived and Directed by Ava Roy Cr
 eated and Performed by Ling Ling Lee Costumes by Brooke Jennings\n\nSlidin
 g-scale tickets: $100-200 via We Players website.\n\nArtist Bios:\n\nAva R
 oy: Ava is the Founding Artistic Director of We Players\, the Bay Area sit
 e-integrated theater company she founded in 2000. For more than two decade
 s\, she has created immersive performances that bring audiences into new r
 elationships with landscape\, history\, and place\, including collaboratio
 ns with the National Park Service\, California State Parks\, and San Franc
 isco Recreation and Parks. Roy is a graduate of Stanford University\, wher
 e she designed her own major\, Ritual and Performance in Aesthetic Educati
 on.\n\nLing Ling Lee: Ling Ling is a San Francisco-born multidisciplinary 
 artist with a background in Chinese theater and dance. A graduate of San F
 rancisco School of the Arts and Shanghai Theatre Academy\, Lee works acros
 s theater\, dance\, storytelling\, and fire performance. Her collaborators
  include We Players\, Meow Wolf\, Disney\, Kaimera Wanderbeasts\, and the 
 European Juggling Convention. With We Players\, Lee has performed as Ox He
 ad/Horse Face in Psychopomp and appeared in Macbeth at Fort Point and Odys
 sea.\n\nBrooke Jennings: Brooke is an award-winning costume designer worki
 ng in the Bay Area and beyond and a graduate of UC Santa Cruz’s Theater 
 Arts master’s program. Her credits include work with San Francisco Playh
 ouse\, Shotgun Players\, San Jose Stage\, Crowded Fire Theater Company\, a
 nd We Players. Jennings began designing for We Players in 2015 with Ondine
  at Sutro\, for which she received a Theater Bay Area Award for Outstandin
 g Costume Design\, and has since become the company’s resident costume d
 esigner and a longtime creative collaborator.
LOCATION:Western Hills Garden
URL:https://www.westernhillsgarden.com/programs/we-players-mountain-goddes
 s-2026
